  <h2 id="timescales">3. One Ratio, Two Timescales: Rhythm $\to$ Pitch &amp; Euclidean Pulses</h2>
  <p>
    Human perception draws a sharp boundary around $\sim 20\text{ Hz}$: below it, periodic impulses are heard as
    <strong>rhythm</strong>; above it, the exact same periodic impulse train fuses into continuous
    <strong>pitch and harmony</strong>. To hear that phase transition honestly — without cheating via a sudden
    oscillator switch — both voices must be generated as <strong>true short impulses (clicks)</strong> whose
    repetition rate accelerates continuously from $2\text{ Hz}$ to $240\text{ Hz}$.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a discrete clock has $n$ ticks and must distribute $k$ firing events as evenly as possible (maximizing
    minimum inter-event spacing, which minimizes peak burst buffer occupancy in a hardware scheduler), the unique
    optimal solution is <strong>Bjorklund's Euclidean algorithm</strong> $E(k, n)$ — the exact same structure as
    Euclid's $\gcd$ and Bresenham's line-drawing algorithm, and the backbone of classical world rhythms like
    $E(3,8)$ (Tresillo) and $E(7,12)$ (West African bell).
  </p>

